{"schema":1,"slug":"chickasaw-hunting-grounds","nation":"Chickasaw","yearStart":1780,"yearEnd":1818,"presenceLabel":"Chickasaw claim in this area","presenceNote":"1780 is the earliest date in the cited sources (Fort Jefferson); 1818 is the cession north of the 35th parallel. Earlier Chickasaw use of the region is not mapped.","confidence":"medium","approximate":true,"overlapNote":"Territories overlapped and shifted. Other nations (Cherokee, Shawnee, Quapaw, Illini, Osage) also hunted and claimed in this region; each frame is a simplified area drawn from treaty river and latitude calls, not a survey.","basis":"Hand-simplified from treaty boundary text (1786, 1805, 1818) and the Royce/BAE 1899 cession maps. Native Land Digital data was not used.","timeline":{"min":1780,"max":1825},"bounds":[[-91.2,34.0],[-87.6,37.4]],"eras":[{"id":"claim","label":"1780–86 · Claimed hunting ground","short":"1780–86","yearStart":1780,"yearEnd":1786,"confidence":"low-medium","sources":["fj","hopewell"],"text":"In 1780 Virginia built Fort Jefferson at the mouth of the Ohio without Chickasaw consent; in 1782 Chickasaw chiefs wrote that the fort stood “in our hunting ground.” The outline is drawn to the later 1786 line, so treat it as the loosest frame.","layers":[{"feature":"r1","style":"soft"}],"mapLabel":"CHICKASAW"},{"id":"hop","label":"1786–1805 · Recognized by treaty","short":"1786","yearStart":1786,"yearEnd":1805,"confidence":"medium","sources":["hopewell","tnenc"],"text":"The Treaty of Hopewell (10 Jan 1786) set lands “allotted to the Chickasaw nation to live and hunt on” from the Cumberland–Tennessee ridge to the Ohio, down the Mississippi. This is the fullest treaty-recognized frame shown.","layers":[{"feature":"r1","style":"solid"}],"mapLabel":"CHICKASAW"},{"id":"c05","label":"1805–1818 · After the 1805 cession","short":"1805","yearStart":1805,"yearEnd":1818,"confidence":"low-medium","sources":["t1805","royce","tngen"],"text":"The 23 Jul 1805 treaty (Royce cession 55) ceded the tract east of the Tennessee River. The hatched strip is that cession; its eastern edge here is a simple midline between the Tennessee and Cumberland rivers, not a surveyed ridge.","layers":[{"feature":"r2","style":"solid"},{"feature":"strip05","style":"hatch"}],"mapLabel":"CHICKASAW"},{"id":"c18","label":"1818 → · After the Jackson Purchase","short":"1818","yearStart":1818,"confidence":"medium","sources":["t1818","jp","royce"],"text":"On 19 Oct 1818 the Chickasaw ceded all claim north of the 35th parallel (Royce cession 100). In Kentucky that is the eight Jackson Purchase counties: no Chickasaw-claimed land remains inside the state on this map.","layers":[{"feature":"r3","style":"solid"},{"feature":"ceded18","style":"hatch"}],"mapLabel":"CEDED 1818","mapSubLabel":"JACKSON PURCHASE"}],"sources":[{"id":"hopewell","label":"Treaty of Hopewell with the Chickasaw, 10 Jan 1786, Art. III","url":"https://treaties.okstate.edu/treaties/treaty-with-the-chickasaw-1786-0014"},{"id":"t1805","label":"Chickasaw treaty, 23 Jul 1805 (Royce cession 55)","url":"https://avalon.law.yale.edu/19th_century/nt003.asp"},{"id":"t1818","label":"Treaty with the Chickasaw, 19 Oct 1818 (Royce cession 100)","url":"https://avalon.law.yale.edu/19th_century/nt006.asp"},{"id":"royce","label":"C. C. Royce, Indian Land Cessions in the United States, BAE 18th Annual Report (1899), Library of Congress","url":"https://www.loc.gov/resource/g3701em.gct00002/"},{"id":"fj","label":"Fort Jefferson (Kentucky): 1780 fort and 1782 Chickasaw letter (Wikipedia)","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fort_Jefferson_(Kentucky)"},{"id":"jp","label":"Jackson Purchase (Wikipedia)","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jackson_Purchase"},{"id":"tnenc","label":"Chickasaws, Tennessee Encyclopedia","url":"https://tennesseeencyclopedia.net/entries/chickasaws/"},{"id":"tngen","label":"Royce cession list (TNGenWeb)","url":"https://www.tngenweb.org/cessions/"}],"geojson":{"type":"FeatureCollection","features":[{"type":"Feature","prop